WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.869
-Our first guest tonight is an
Emmy-nominated actor you know

00:00:02.869 --> 00:00:05.205
from films such as
"A Beautiful Mind"

00:00:05.205 --> 00:00:06.472
and the Avengers franchise.

00:00:06.472 --> 00:00:10.776
He stars as Andy Warhol
in "The Collaboration,"

00:00:10.776 --> 00:00:12.878
which opens in
Manhattan Theatre Club's

00:00:12.878 --> 00:00:14.881
Samuel J. Friedman Theatre
on December 20th.

00:00:14.881 --> 00:00:16.182
Please welcome back to the show
our friend

00:00:16.182 --> 00:00:17.417
Paul Bettany, everybody!

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:19.452
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:19.452 --> 00:00:27.126 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:27.126 --> 00:00:34.833 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:36.669
Hello!
-Hello!

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.538
-I am so happy to have you here.

00:00:38.538 --> 00:00:40.472
-Lovely and happy to be here.

00:00:40.472 --> 00:00:42.842
-And you're on stage right now.

00:00:42.842 --> 00:00:44.177
You've got a show tonight?

00:00:44.177 --> 00:00:45.544
-Yeah, I have a show tonight.

00:00:45.544 --> 00:00:47.746
-And this is -- you get to play
Andy Warhol in this.

00:00:47.746 --> 00:00:49.248
-I do, yeah.
-It's about a collaboration with

00:00:49.248 --> 00:00:52.152
Andy Warhol and his friendship
with Basquiat, '80s New York.

00:00:52.152 --> 00:00:53.919
This is a role you originally
turned down.

00:00:53.919 --> 00:00:58.290
-Yeah, I turned it down on --
Yeah, I thought maybe

00:00:58.290 --> 00:01:01.660
there's a reason that
Andy Warhol is always a cameo.

00:01:01.660 --> 00:01:02.896
-Right.
-You know?

00:01:02.896 --> 00:01:04.597
Well, maybe there is a reason,
you know?

00:01:04.597 --> 00:01:05.899
We'll see what the critics say.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.533
[ Laughter ]

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:12.905
But the producer, when I turned
it down, sent me his diaries

00:01:12.905 --> 00:01:17.510
and his diaries were dictated to
his assistant, Pat,

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:21.280
and they're incredibly these
long, circuitous sentences,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.083
and he sounds more like
Truman Capote

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.585
than the sort of monosyllabic --
-Yeah, that's true.

00:01:26.585 --> 00:01:29.188
In films we tend to see him
floating through the background,

00:01:29.188 --> 00:01:30.623
one or two lines
and then he's off.

00:01:30.623 --> 00:01:33.493
-So we'll see if it works.

00:01:33.493 --> 00:01:36.429
-Very exciting.
Now, one thing that has to work

00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:38.398
as well is you, every night,

00:01:38.398 --> 00:01:40.366
physically have to go up
and do the show.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:42.502
Are you regretting the choice
to return to the theater?

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:44.571
-Well, yeah, yes, yes.
-[ Laughter ]

00:01:44.571 --> 00:01:47.373
It's been 25 years.
-Has it been 25 years?

00:01:47.373 --> 00:01:49.775
Wow.
-Yes, since I've been on stage.

00:01:49.775 --> 00:01:53.479
And I kind of got the -- When we
did "WandaVision," we did

00:01:53.479 --> 00:01:56.181
one episode that was in front of
a live studio audience.

00:01:56.181 --> 00:01:58.851
And people were laughing at
my jokes. Not my jokes.

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:00.219
But they were laughing at
my jokes.

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:02.255
Somebody else had written them.
Someone cleverer than me.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:03.623
-But that's how the theater
works too, right?

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:06.225
-That's right.
And I'm really shallow.

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:09.062
-[ Laughter ]
-It immediately appealed to me.

00:02:09.062 --> 00:02:10.296
-Oh, yeah,
you get a taste of it.

00:02:10.296 --> 00:02:11.330
-I loved it.
-Oh, I know.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:12.665
-I was like,
"I gotta to do that."

00:02:12.665 --> 00:02:14.567
So I did it
and it's a nightmare.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:15.968
[ Laughter ]

00:02:15.968 --> 00:02:17.269
I thought it's going to
be great,

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:19.539
no more early film mornings,
you know.

00:02:19.539 --> 00:02:21.808
Getting up at the dawn
of your crack.

00:02:21.808 --> 00:02:23.543
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.543 --> 00:02:26.946
And then the truth is
it's really hard.

00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:29.315
-[ Laughs ] Yeah.
-And really frightening.

00:02:29.315 --> 00:02:33.052
And you have to learn all of
these words at the same time

00:02:33.052 --> 00:02:35.088
and then go out
in front of people.

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:37.657
It's been a disaster, yeah.

00:02:37.657 --> 00:02:38.758
-Yeah.
-[ Laughter ]

00:02:38.758 --> 00:02:40.359
This is going to be
very inspirational

00:02:40.359 --> 00:02:42.128
for young actors who are
watching, I think.

00:02:42.128 --> 00:02:44.430
[ Laughter ]
-Oh, yeah, no, that's what

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:47.066
I'm hoping -- I've convinced,
you know, all of my children

00:02:47.066 --> 00:02:49.068
not to be in the --
-Yeah.

00:02:49.068 --> 00:02:50.603
And do you think
that's going to hold?

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.004
You have three kids.

00:02:52.004 --> 00:02:54.841
And I guess you're almost --
Two have sort of committed.

00:02:54.841 --> 00:02:57.677
You've got an 11-year-old.
So you really think there's

00:02:57.677 --> 00:02:58.845
no chance she's going to --

00:02:58.845 --> 00:03:00.580
-Well, the jury's still out
on her.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:04.250
But the older one,
he's an aeronautical engineer.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:05.584
-Seems such a better choice
than acting.

00:03:05.584 --> 00:03:09.989
-Yeah, mainly because
biologically he's not my son.

00:03:09.989 --> 00:03:11.457
That's the reason he's
an aeronautical engineer.

00:03:11.457 --> 00:03:12.959
[ Laughter ]

00:03:12.959 --> 00:03:15.628
'Cause he doesn't have
my math gene.

00:03:15.628 --> 00:03:17.663
-They almost didn't give him
the job at the last minute

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:19.399
until he was like,
"No, no, he's not my dad-dad."

00:03:19.399 --> 00:03:22.835
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:26.239
-Paul Bettany? Yeah, exactly.

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.607
But yeah. Yeah. I'm 50.

00:03:28.607 --> 00:03:31.210
I thought, winter's coming,
my joints aren't going to

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:33.412
be up to those early mornings.
I'll do some theater.

00:03:33.412 --> 00:03:34.547
And it's been a nightmare.

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:36.081
-Have you had --
-[ Laughter ]

00:03:36.081 --> 00:03:37.283
50 is a big number.

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:39.519
I'm coming up -- That will be
next year for me.

00:03:39.519 --> 00:03:42.454
What about -- Have there been
other things

00:03:42.454 --> 00:03:43.523
that have made you
really, like --

00:03:43.523 --> 00:03:44.624
-Sure, I know what you're
talking about.

00:03:44.624 --> 00:03:45.825
-Yeah, I am. Okay.

00:03:45.825 --> 00:03:48.360
-It's very important,
got to do it.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:49.662
It's called a colonoscopy.

00:03:49.662 --> 00:03:50.830
-Yeah, have you?
-Yes.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:52.097
-Did you take the leap?

00:03:52.097 --> 00:03:53.833
-Oh, I did it. I did it.

00:03:53.833 --> 00:03:57.070
And I'm now going to tell you
about it on television.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:58.570
[ Laughter ]

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.205
-This is an education.

00:04:00.205 --> 00:04:02.541
There's two things I want people
to take away from our time here.

00:04:02.541 --> 00:04:05.044
It's get a colonoscopy
if you're a man who's 50.

00:04:05.044 --> 00:04:06.946
And that acting is a drag.

00:04:06.946 --> 00:04:09.248
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:11.917
-But not as much as a nightmare
as a colonoscopy.

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:13.753
-Well, that's good. So after you
got your colonoscopy,

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:15.988
you're like, "Oh, doing a
Broadway show is not that bad."

00:04:15.988 --> 00:04:17.122
[ Laughter ]

00:04:17.122 --> 00:04:19.391
-Well...

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:20.860
Okay. So here's the thing.

00:04:20.860 --> 00:04:22.562
I was very frightened.
-Yeah?

00:04:22.562 --> 00:04:24.697
-The doctor was -- I said,
"I don't want to go under."

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:25.798
I'm very frightened of
going under.

00:04:25.798 --> 00:04:27.166
-Oh, you don't like anesthesia?

00:04:27.166 --> 00:04:28.467
-I said, "I don't want to do
that."

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:30.470
And so he said, "Well, don't
worry about it.

00:04:30.470 --> 00:04:32.338
It's like --
It's a keyhole camera."

00:04:32.338 --> 00:04:34.507
Keyhole is the word
that he used.

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:35.374
[ Laughter ]

00:04:35.374 --> 00:04:37.510
And I went into the room.

00:04:37.510 --> 00:04:39.611
And they were all --
There was this huge --

00:04:39.611 --> 00:04:41.480
Well, like lots of people.

00:04:41.480 --> 00:04:44.016
And I thought --
I mean, let me say a crew.

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:46.152
-Uh-Huh, yeah.
-Like a film crew.

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:47.553
-Right, sure.

00:04:47.553 --> 00:04:50.289
-And this -- You would have
to climb a beanstalk

00:04:50.289 --> 00:04:52.391
to find a keyhole this big.

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:55.294
Because I looked at it.
It was like an IMAX camera.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:59.599
And I immediately said,
"Put me under right now.

00:04:59.599 --> 00:05:03.636
Just put me under.
Have you got a boom operator?"

00:05:03.636 --> 00:05:05.804
[ Laughter ]

00:05:05.804 --> 00:05:08.908
So I go -- They put me under.
I wake up.

00:05:08.908 --> 00:05:12.311
I'm high as a kite on pethidine,
and just, like, so happy.

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:14.647
And I turn around.
I'm in the recovery room.

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:18.051
There are these two nurses, and
I started flirting with them

00:05:18.051 --> 00:05:19.886
and they're laughing at
all my jokes

00:05:19.886 --> 00:05:22.555
because I'm so funny and witty
and clever.

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:24.757
And there's this really annoying
buzzing noise,

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:27.092
but it doesn't matter.
It's just a great day.

00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:28.694
I'm flirting
and they're laughing.

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:30.196
And then I turn around
and I see my wife.

00:05:30.196 --> 00:05:31.297
And I go, "Uh-oh."

00:05:31.297 --> 00:05:32.765
[ Laughter ]

00:05:32.765 --> 00:05:36.269
But she's laughing.
She's laughing too.

00:05:36.269 --> 00:05:38.204
And I'm thinking, "Oh, my God,
I was flirting with these --

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:39.872
But this is the best day
of my life.

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.407
I've had a colonoscopy.

00:05:41.407 --> 00:05:43.042
I've been flirting with
these girls.

00:05:43.042 --> 00:05:44.544
Everybody's laughing at me.

00:05:44.544 --> 00:05:45.711
What the [bleep] is that noise?"

00:05:45.711 --> 00:05:47.613
And I started listening
and I realized

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.315
I'd been farting
for three minutes.

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:51.784
[ Laughter ]

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:56.088
Because -- Yeah, because --

00:05:56.088 --> 00:05:57.923
They have to inflate you.

00:05:57.923 --> 00:05:59.592
They probably don't do it
like that.

00:05:59.592 --> 00:06:00.826
[ Laughter ]

00:06:00.826 --> 00:06:02.928
-Yeah, they have a super
high-tech camera

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:04.664
and then, like, just
a basketball pump.

00:06:04.664 --> 00:06:07.900
-I don't know.
I was unconscious. Maybe.

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:09.735
And so they inflate you.

00:06:09.735 --> 00:06:12.137
And then -- Because they got to
get this IMAX camera

00:06:12.137 --> 00:06:14.272
and the whole crew in.
-Yeah.

00:06:14.272 --> 00:06:15.975
The crew goes in as well?

00:06:15.975 --> 00:06:17.843
-I don't know.
I was unconscious.

00:06:17.843 --> 00:06:19.579
Who knows what happened?

00:06:19.579 --> 00:06:22.047
-I have a great deal more
to ask you about.

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:23.415
Thank you for sharing
that story.

00:06:23.415 --> 00:06:26.152
It's a beautiful
Christmas story.

00:06:26.152 --> 00:06:28.721
We'll be right back with more
from Paul Bettany.

